[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: Application-layer services on the Office B server (srv1), specifically the HTTP service (Apache).
- Mechanism: From PC1, the network path to srv1 is healthy (ICMP succeeds: 0% loss, ~2 ms RTT), but TCP connections to the service ports fail because nothing is listening. Curl from PC1 and PC3 to 10.5.0.4:80 returns “Connection refused,” which occurs when the host immediately resets SYNs due to a closed port. Some client apps surface this as a “timeout.”
- Localization: On srv1 itself. Evidence: netstat on srv1 shows only named (DNS) listening on 10.5.0.4:53 and 127.0.0.1:953; there is no listener on TCP/80 (or other common server ports). systemctl reports apache2 is “loaded … enabled” but “inactive (dead),” and nginx is not running. This confirms the web service is down at the host, not in the network.
- Root cause: The Apache HTTP service on srv1 was stopped/disabled during last night’s changes, leaving no listener on TCP/80.
- Corrective fix: Restart and ensure persistence of the intended service(s) on srv1. For the web UI: systemctl start apache2; then verify with netstat/ss that 10.5.0.4:80 is listening and confirm access from PC1/Office B. If Apache was intentionally replaced, re-enable the correct daemon (e.g., nginx) or restore the previous configuration. Also confirm there’s no conflicting firewall policy, though current evidence shows port closure rather than filtering.

=== END ===